Falsifies `intrusive_queue::verification::extract_if_splits_the_queue_in_order`.

The defect: the walk reads the next link after it handles a node, not before. That reads as the
natural loop order, and it is right for every node the filter passes over. A node the filter picks
has already left by then, and leaving cleared its link, so the walk stops at the first pick and
never shows the filter the nodes behind it.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(seen, count, "the filter missed a node")`, whenever
the filter picks a node that has another node behind it.

diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -379,7 +379,6 @@ impl<T: Node> Queue<T> {
         while let Some(node) = cursor {
             // SAFETY: as in `remove`. The link is read before `node` can leave, because leaving
             // clears it.
-            cursor = unsafe { (*node.as_ptr()).next() };
             if filter(node) {
                 // SAFETY: `node` is on this queue, and `previous` is the last node the walk kept,
                 // which is the node just before it.
@@ -387,6 +386,7 @@ impl<T: Node> Queue<T> {
             } else {
                 previous = Some(node);
             }
+            cursor = unsafe { (*node.as_ptr()).next() };
         }
         extracted
